  "timing": {
    "//": "Timing for the modelled part. This file carries the SHAPE of the machine -- how many compute units and channels, how wide a cache line is, how big the register file is -- and nothing that had to be measured. Latencies, issue occupancies, port counts, launch costs and the composition's calibration factors are measurements against hardware; they are not in this repository and they arrive as an overlay, which mirage merges with --timing-tuning or which tests/corpus/run-meter-emulated.sh --tuning merges for a direct run. Without one, every calibrated parameter resolves to the slowest reasonable value for that parameter, the calibration factors resolve to neutral, and the run reports which ones it had to guess: an uncalibrated run reads slow and looks suspicious, never fast and accurate.",
    "enabled": true,
    "clock_mhz": 2100,
    "machine": {
      "//calibration": "The four scales below and straggler_cycles are the composition's calibration factors, and they are neutral here. Each exists because a term is right in shape and wrong in size for a reason the model cannot yet name: stall_exposed_fraction is how much of a wavefront's modelled memory stall is really exposed, latency_exposure_scale and fill_exposure_scale scale the two halves of the latency term, fill_ramp_scale scales the machine-fill ramp, and straggler_cycles is how far a dispatch's slowest wavefront runs past its average one per doubling of the wavefront count. They are named rather than folded into the parameters they scale so that a reader can tell a fitted number from a measured one.",
